WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:06.190
We've all shed a tear or two watching a film
— though some of us may never admit it.

00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:10.490
If you're looking to let it all out over some
particularly poignant films, you've come to

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:11.870
the right place.

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:16.519
Bring some tissues for your issues — and
beware of spoilers! — as we take a look

00:00:16.519 --> 00:00:19.390
at some movies sure to make you cry.

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:20.529
The Iron Giant

00:00:20.529 --> 00:00:26.449
This 1999 animated classic tells the story
of young Hogarth Hughes and his metal-eating

00:00:26.449 --> 00:00:32.009
buddy as they embark on an exciting adventure
in suburban Maine in the late 1950s.

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:37.510
Fueled by Cold War paranoia, the U.S. military
thinks the gentle Iron Giant is a threat and

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:41.530
launches a nuclear missile at him — and
Hogarth's hometown.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:47.230
The lovable hunk of metal, minimally but effectively
voiced by Vin Diesel, makes the ultimate sacrifice

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:52.800
to save the town and his new friends … but
that alone won't make you blink back tears.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.430
Here's the kicker: having previously bonded
over Superman comics with Hogarth, the Iron

00:00:57.430 --> 00:01:02.510
Giant imagines himself as the Man of Steel
as he soars through the sky, moments before

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:04.100
the nuke tears him apart:

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:07.300
"Superman!"

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:15.930
If a cartoon's never made you cry, this could
be your kryptonite.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:17.390
Guardians of the Galaxy

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:23.210
2014's magnificent addition to the Marvel
Cinematic Universe, Guardians of the Galaxy

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:28.270
earned a ton of praise for its charming performances,
its tongue-in-cheek take on the superhero

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:30.659
genre, and its bangin' soundtrack.

00:01:30.659 --> 00:01:32.840
But it's also a major tearjerker.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:38.240
Like the Iron Giant before him, giant tree
dude Groot — also minimally yet effectively

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:42.829
voiced by Vin Diesel — sacrifices himself
to save his friends by covering them with

00:01:42.829 --> 00:01:45.020
an unbreakable Groot-sphere.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:49.840
But the tears really start flowing when Rocket
asks Groot why he would give his life for

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:54.979
theirs, prompting a poignant twist on the
big guy's signature three-word mantra:

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:59.860
"We are Groot."

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:03.020
Who knew trees had such big hearts?

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:07.380
And who knew Vin Diesel acted better when
you gave him fewer words to say?

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:08.380
Up

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:12.700
Like the brightly colored balloons lifting
Carl's house, you probably saw this one coming

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:14.190
from a mile away.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:18.720
Up is notorious for its unbelievably sad and
sweet opening sequence.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:23.780
You go in expecting a quirky tale of a cranky
old man, his floating house, and his chubby

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:27.910
boy scout buddy — and then get socked in
the stomach with sentiment.

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:32.310
Pixar proves it's much more than that twee
tale right from the get-go, offering up a

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:37.260
sprawling love story in miniature, as Carl
meets Ellie, the love of his life, and we

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:41.280
get to see their entire relationship play
out — up to Ellie's death:

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:52.140
That's all in the first ten minutes.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:55.220
Thanks for making us cry into our popcorn,
Pixar.

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:57.460
The Shawshank Redemption

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:02.700
This widely beloved 1994 flick is best known
for the relationship between convicts Andy

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:07.780
and Red, and Andy's elaborate, decades-in-the-making
escape plan.

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:13.010
But it's the story of elderly convict Brooks
Hatlen, released on parole after fifty years

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:16.460
behind bars, that will have you clutching
the Kleenex.

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:20.420
Despite his best efforts, Hatlen just can't
adjust to life on the outside.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:22.830
"I don't like it here.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:25.970
I'm tired of being afraid all the time.

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:29.200
I've decided not to stay."

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:34.590
Hatlen's entire farewell monologue is during
an ugly-cry-inducing scene — ending in his

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:35.890
suicide.

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:40.500
Just before he hangs himself in his apartment,
he carves "Brooks was here" into the ceiling

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:41.500
beam.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:43.070
Field of Dreams

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:47.840
There's nothing quite like a moving father-son
scene, but it's made even more powerful in

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.040
a film like Field of Dreams.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:54.730
After receiving visions and hearing voices
in his head, Iowa corn farmer Ray Kinsella

00:03:54.730 --> 00:04:00.250
constructs a baseball diamond that magically
draws in one very special player: Ray's dead

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:01.550
father, John.

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:05.810
We won't give away too much of the context,
but the pain of Ray's journey to see his father

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:10.540
once more and to have him beam with pride
will reduce you to a puddle of tears.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:15.270
As with most films on our list, a single line
will stab you right in the chest:

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:16.800
"You wanna have a catch?"

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:21.680
"I'd like that."

00:04:21.689 --> 00:04:24.210
And … cue the ugly man-tears.

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:25.540
The LEGO Movie

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:30.639
Yes, a movie about plastic dudes battling
an evil Will Ferrell is a tearjerker.

00:04:30.639 --> 00:04:34.199
And like Field of Dreams, it's all about daddy
issues.

00:04:34.199 --> 00:04:39.370
In an unexpectedly moving twist, the imagination
of a real-life boy, Finn, is revealed to be

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:43.340
responsible for the brick-based hijinks of
the animated story.

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:49.030
Finn's father realizes his son's made-up antagonist,
President Business, is actually based on him,

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:52.460
his perfectionism, and his less-than-stellar
parenting skills.

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:56.900
He asks Finn what his protagonist, Emmet,
would say to President Business, and the response

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:59.050
will punch you right in the feelings.

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:06.180
"You are the most talented, most interesting,
and most extraordinary person in the universe."

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:10.479
The tears really start to flow when Finn tells
his father "You can change everything."

00:05:10.479 --> 00:05:12.129
Ooh boy.

00:05:12.129 --> 00:05:13.129
Time to call dad.

00:05:13.129 --> 00:05:14.639
Thanks for watching!

00:05:14.639 --> 00:05:17.370
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:20.209
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

